#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "74ffb6be-e709-5635-b6e2-98911c40a19e" :question/math-academy-id "e-184" :question/problem "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/id #uuid "a2bb6a33-8a57-5b6b-9d35-cf611fbd5c9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-185" :question/problem "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b00f1213-cdaf-512c-9842-92cbee1ec63a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6100" :question/problem "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8304b514-754e-54d2-b053-31a4e39c33dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-2400" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018421ea-dca9-5daf-ab3a-c27696ce4249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288034} {:answer/id #uuid "1e02db35-b595-59ea-973e-bd157371128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288223} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5c0bc2-0072-5944-8dd3-fe8dcbae8938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288693} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7a7d1a-20e3-5bd6-9408-f184d8d4065a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45efbdd-39dd-5ca7-b621-baab9c7920f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3d7d091-4a85-557a-b384-1cb4715f620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2403" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}})(2x)-(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})(2)}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2089f74f-7bb1-531f-9f54-4e2969f07e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360974} {:answer/id #uuid "54599985-5dd3-502a-b9ef-88b1d618a930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361211} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddff39-ee62-599f-a890-d26c22490faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361340} {:answer/id #uuid "93d239b8-90ad-5454-b0f7-400662e44c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03caba5-0350-5e9d-aa7c-ebd7a638e046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25941eb3-76ad-51ee-8456-c5400a4c8aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-9944" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={sin}^{3}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)({sin}^{3}⁡x)+({x}^{2})(3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{sin}^{3}⁡x+3{x}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=0} & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡(0)+3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
